{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.DiscriminantCurveInQ","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.DiscriminantCurveInQ","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TraceRealSpectrumComplex"],"declarations":25,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_order_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_order_three","kind":"theorem","line":218,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L218-L225","doc":"The three-cycle really does have order 3, so `2π/3` is not an accident of the\nnumerals: `l³ = 1` and `l ≠ 1` force `l² + l + 1 = 0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_cos_eigenphase","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_cos_eigenphase","kind":"theorem","line":209,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L209-L216","doc":"The eigenphase in the Step-22 convention, `θ = arccos (tr / (2√det))`, is\n`arccos (−1/2) = 2π/3` — because `det = 1` and `tr = −1`. Recorded as the\n`cos` identity so it needs no `arccos` API. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_disc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_disc","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L206-L207","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_isElliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_isElliptic","kind":"theorem","line":201,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L201-L204","doc":"The limiting operator is **elliptic**, with `disc = −3`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_cube_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_cube_root","kind":"theorem","line":197,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L197-L199","doc":"Hence `l³ = 1`: the limiting eigenvalues are cube roots of unity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classical_charpoly","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classical_charpoly","kind":"theorem","line":188,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L188-L195","doc":"`L² + L + 1 = 0` on the invariants: the characteristic polynomial of the\nleading operator is `λ² + λ + 1`, whose roots are the primitive cube roots of\nunity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classicalDet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classicalDet","kind":"def","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L186-L186","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.classicalTr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.classicalTr","kind":"def","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L178-L185","doc":"The leading direction of `Δ_L` as `q → 1`. Every NF coordinate carries a\nfactor `(q−1)(q+1)`; the coordinates vanishing to first order are `γ` and `L₋`,\nso the direction is `−(1 + T₁T₀)`, and at `q = 1` that is the concrete matrix\n`!![−1/2, √3/2; −√3/2, −1/2]` — rotation by `2π/3`, negated.\n\nCarried through its invariants rather than as a matrix, since only `tr` and `det`\nenter the eigenphase. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.discLong_eq_quotient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.discLong_eq_quotient","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L169-L174","doc":"`disc_L(x) = R(x²)`. So the genus-8 curve `y² = disc_L(x)` covers the genus-4\ncurve `y² = R(w)`. Neither is genus 1 — there is no elliptic curve here, hence no\ntower of elliptic curves indexed by anything. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.discQuotient","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.discQuotient","kind":"def","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L167-L167","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.tildeA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.tildeA","kind":"def","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L163-L165","doc":"`R(w) = (w − 4)·(Ã(w)² − 4w)/16` with `Ã(w) = 2w⁴ − 21w³ + 77w² − 115w + 58`\n— the degree-9 polynomial in `w = x²` that `disc_L` factors through. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.discLong_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.discLong_even","kind":"theorem","line":158,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L158-L161","doc":"`disc_L` is **even in `x`**: the two degree-8 factors swap under `x ↦ −x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.discLong_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.discLong_factor","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L151-L156","doc":"The two degree-8 factors multiply to `A² − 4x²`, so the whole discriminant is\n`(x² − 4)(A(x)² − 4x²)/16`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.discLong","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.discLong","kind":"def","line":147,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L147-L149","doc":"`disc_L(x) = (x − 2)(x + 2)·G₋(x)·G₊(x)/16`, the long model's spectral\ndiscriminant in the bar-invariant coordinate. Degree 18. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.gPlus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.gPlus","kind":"def","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L145-L145","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.gMinus","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.gMinus","kind":"def","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L143-L144","doc":"`G∓(x) = A(x) ∓ 2x`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.bigA","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.bigA","kind":"def","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L139-L141","doc":"`A(x) = 2x⁸ − 21x⁶ + 77x⁴ − 115x² + 58`, the even part of the two degree-8\nfactors of the long model's discriminant. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortDisc_in_x","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortDisc_in_x","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L130-L135","doc":"In the coordinate `x = q + 1/q` the same discriminant is the conic\n`x² − 4`, which is why `y² = disc_S` has genus `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortModel_not_elliptic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortModel_not_elliptic","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L123-L128","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortModelDisc_nonneg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortModelDisc_nonneg","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L121-L121","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortModelDisc_eq_hSq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortModelDisc_eq_hSq","kind":"theorem","line":114,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L114-L119","doc":"**The short model is hyperbolic at every real `q`.** `disc_S = h² ≥ 0`, with\nequality only at `q = ±1`, so `Δ_S` never has a complex-conjugate spectrum and\ncan never carry a phase — for any `q`, not just the substrate `q₀`. Steps 21–25\nsampled this at one point and could not have said so. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortDet_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortDet_eq","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L90-L112","doc":"`det Δ_S = −h²/4`, and hence `disc = tr² − 4·det = h²`.\n\nWritten on the block's entries: with `α = h²/2`, `β = −h/2` and `M = T₀ + T₁`,\n`det (α·I + β·M) = α² + α·β·tr M + β²·det M`, `tr M = 2h`, and\n`det M = (q + a)(−1/q + h − a) − b²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortTr_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortTr_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L83-L88","doc":"`tr Δ_S = 0` identically in `q` — `T₀` and `T₁` both have trace `h`, and the\n`γ` coefficient `h²/2` cancels them exactly. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.shortTr","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.shortTr","kind":"def","line":73,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L73-L81","doc":"The `(2,1)` block of the short-model decay operator\n`Δ_S = (h²/2)·γ − (h/2)·σ₀ − (h/2)·σ₁`, written out entry by entry.\n\n`T₀ = diag(q, −1/q)` and `T₁ = !![a, b; b, h − a]` with `a = −1/(q³ + q)`; the\noff-diagonal `b` enters only through `b²`, so it is carried as a hypothesis and\nno square root appears. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.two_le_add_inv_of_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.two_le_add_inv_of_pos","kind":"theorem","line":63,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/DiscriminantCurveInQ.lean#L63-L69","doc":"`x = q + 1/q ≥ 2` for every `q > 0`. This is why `disc_S = x² − 4 ≥ 0` is an\ninequality about the whole real branch rather than a fact about `q₀`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}